use std
use os
into haven-release.he
lang he

`haven-release feed <dir> <version> <notes file>` writes the Tauri updater feed for a Haven Desktop release, and `haven-release amnios <amni-os checkout> <deb file> <version>` publishes that release's .deb to the Amni OS package repo. Every line it prints starts with `haven-release: `.

`json_text(s const byte[:]) byte[>..]` returns s as a JSON string with its surrounding double quotes: backslash, double quote, newline, carriage return and tab are escaped with a backslash, other bytes below 0x20 become `\u00XX`, and every other byte is copied as it is.

`feed(dir const byte[:], version const byte[:], notes_path const byte[:]) int` handles the feed command and returns the exit code. The five feed entries, in this order, as target then file name, are: `windows-x86_64` and `Haven_<version>_x64-setup.exe`; `linux-x86_64` and `Haven_<version>_amd64.AppImage`; `linux-x86_64-deb` and `Haven_<version>_amd64.deb`; `darwin-aarch64` and `Haven.app.tar.gz`; `darwin-x86_64` and `Haven.app.tar.gz`. For each entry the file dir + "/" + name must read, and the file dir + "/" + name + ".sig" must read and be non-empty once trimmed. When any of them is missing it prints `missing <name>` or `missing signature for <name>` and returns 1 without writing anything. The notes file must read and be non-empty once trimmed, otherwise it prints `missing notes` and returns 1. Otherwise it writes the file dir + "/latest.json" using `write_file_atomic` with exactly this shape, using two-space JSON indentation and one trailing newline: an object with `version`, `notes` (the trimmed notes), `pub_date` (the current UTC time written `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.000Z`, computed from `time()` with the civil-from-days formula) and `platforms`, an object with each target in the order above whose value is an object with `signature` (the trimmed signature text) and `url` (`https://github.com/Amnibro/Haven-Desktop/releases/download/v<version>-tauri/<name>`). Every string goes through json_text. It prints `wrote <dir>/latest.json` and returns 0.

`amnios(checkout const byte[:], deb const byte[:], version const byte[:]) int` handles the amnios command and returns the exit code. P is checkout + "/packaging/haven-desktop" and F is P + "/PKGBUILD". Each shell command below runs with `run_command`; when one exits non-zero it prints `failed: <command>` and returns 1. It gets the SHA-256 of the deb by running `sha256sum '<deb>' > '<P>/.deb.sha256'`, reading that file and taking its first word, then deletes that file. It reads F and rewrites it line by line, keeping every other line unchanged, with the line starting `pkgver=` becoming `pkgver=<version>`, the line starting `pkgrel=` becoming `pkgrel=1`, and the line starting `sha256sums=` becoming `sha256sums=('<sha>')`, then saves it with `write_file_atomic`. When any of those three lines is missing it prints `PKGBUILD is missing a field` and returns 1 before saving. Then it runs, in order: `cp '<deb>' '<P>/Haven_<version>_amd64.deb'`; `rm -rf '<checkout>/out/haven-desktop-repo' && mkdir -p '<checkout>/out/haven-desktop-repo'`; `cd '<P>' && PKGEXT=.pkg.tar PKGDEST='<checkout>/out/haven-desktop-repo' makepkg -f --nodeps --noconfirm`; `AMNI_LOCAL_REPO='<checkout>/out/haven-desktop-repo' '<checkout>/tools/publish-repo.sh'`; `rm -rf '<P>/src' '<P>/pkg' '<P>/Haven_<version>_amd64.deb' '<checkout>/out/haven-desktop-repo'`. It prints `published haven-desktop <version>-1 to the Amni OS repo` and returns 0.

`main()` reads `args()`, whose element 0 is the program path. When element 1 is `feed` and there are exactly 5 elements it exits with `feed` of elements 2, 3 and 4. When element 1 is `amnios` and there are exactly 5 elements it exits with `amnios` of elements 2, 3 and 4. Otherwise it prints `usage: haven-release feed <dir> <version> <notes file> | amnios <amni-os checkout> <deb file> <version>` and exits 2.
